{"kind":"assert","text":"Follow-up work discovered while repairing CreateConfig, deliberately left out of that head: the custody contract in docs/reference/architecture.md does not name the refusal class the repair introduces.\n\nThe repair publishes the configuration by hard-linking a private staging file to the destination. A filesystem that cannot hard-link - exFAT or FAT32 removable media, some SMB and FUSE mounts - therefore refuses attach permanently, where before it succeeded. gs init is unaffected, because it publishes through rename.\n\nThe contract as written still holds. The page says no platform gets a different fallback and that the writer reports the failure and the writable configuration does not proceed, which is exactly what the repair does: os.Link has one call site, its error is returned unchanged, and there is no rename fallback, no error-class branching and no retry. The clause naming a non-Unix platform holding the file is illustrative, not an enumeration, so nothing on the page is now false.\n\nWhat is missing is that a reader of that paragraph cannot learn that attach and init now differ in which filesystems they can write to. I did not change the page in the repair head, because that would add a second artifact path to a CI repair and collide with ox-builders live artifact at docs/reference/architecture.md. Someone should own that documentation change separately.\n\nI have not tested the refusal on a real filesystem without hard links; the claim is read from os.Link's single call site and its unbranched error path, not measured."}